1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,186
Between December 2025 and November 2026,

2
00:00:03,226 --> 00:00:07,141
six papers closed in on one answer from three directions.

3
00:00:07,181 --> 00:00:09,847
The bottleneck in ontology construction

4
00:00:09,887 --> 00:00:12,970
moved from who writes it to who can prove it.

5
00:00:14,010 --> 00:00:16,236
The benchmark came first.

6
00:00:16,276 --> 00:00:20,768
Burr binds evaluation to table and column mappings

7
00:00:20,808 --> 00:00:24,756
across 54 scenarios and four real databases.

8
00:00:24,796 --> 00:00:29,469
On the micro-benchmark Gemini 2.5 Pro leads at 0.60,

9
00:00:29,509 --> 00:00:31,645
GPT-4.1 follows at 0.53,

10
00:00:31,685 --> 00:00:36,086
and the rule-based systems land at 0.66 and 0.45.

11
00:00:36,126 --> 00:00:39,712
On real databases the authors' own words

12
00:00:39,752 --> 00:00:42,975
are that all systems perform poorly.

13
00:00:43,015 --> 00:00:45,694
Rules beat every model on NPD,

14
00:00:45,734 --> 00:00:49,410
and nothing passed the enterprise schema.

15
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:51,994
The gap came next.

16
00:00:52,034 --> 00:00:56,658
A systematic review counted 30 papers and 41 studies.

17
00:00:56,698 --> 00:01:00,794
Implementation takes 27, maintenance takes one.

18
00:01:00,834 --> 00:01:05,810
Eleven studies ran no experiment, nine ran no evaluation.

19
00:01:05,850 --> 00:01:08,186
One layer up it gets worse.

20
00:01:08,226 --> 00:01:10,650
OntoAxiom ran twelve models,

21
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:12,762
subclass averages 0.359,

22
00:01:12,802 --> 00:01:15,050
domain 0.038, range 0.030,

23
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:18,570
and changing the prompt hardly moves it.

24
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:23,346
What decides the score is the vocabulary.

25
00:01:23,386 --> 00:01:26,662
Thirteen teams entered LLMs4OL 2026.

26
00:01:26,702 --> 00:01:30,438
On Reuse, where a vocabulary is supplied,

27
00:01:30,478 --> 00:01:32,740
five of eight clear 0.85.

28
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:35,688
On Flagship, with no vocabulary,

29
00:01:35,728 --> 00:01:39,740
graph similarity runs from 0.4167 to 0.7762.

30
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:41,582
One team, one model,

31
00:01:41,622 --> 00:01:46,463
the exact-to-semantic gap is 0.0212 with a vocabulary

32
00:01:46,503 --> 00:01:48,213
and 0.2063 without.

33
00:01:48,253 --> 00:01:52,450
Non-taxonomic relations sit near zero in both.

34
00:01:53,490 --> 00:01:56,323
The acceptance machinery exists.

35
00:01:56,363 --> 00:01:58,927
Forty-one pitfalls from OOPS,

36
00:01:58,967 --> 00:02:04,583
six hundred and sixty-five flaws cut to single digits by RIGOR,

37
00:02:04,623 --> 00:02:09,163
an axiom benchmark, and 1,393 competency questions.

38
00:02:09,203 --> 00:02:10,779
Four holes remain.

39
00:02:10,819 --> 00:02:17,692
Non-taxonomic relations, domain and range, maintenance, and lexical fidelity.

40
00:02:17,732 --> 00:02:20,296
The divide is not model size.

41
00:02:20,336 --> 00:02:22,810
It is where acceptance sits.
